# Hark needs two kinds of access to the input stack, and this rule grants the
# half that is not already granted by default.
#
# READING keyboards (/dev/input/event*) is how Hark sees the push-to-talk
# chord. Those nodes are already root:input 0660 on every mainstream distro,
# so membership of the `input` group is all that is required and this file
# does not touch them.
#
# WRITING /dev/uinput is how Hark pastes: under Wayland there is no XTEST, so
# the Ctrl+V that ends a dictation is synthesized through a virtual keyboard.
# The kernel creates /dev/uinput as root:root 0600, which no desktop session
# can open. This rule hands it to the same `input` group, so one group
# membership covers both directions.
#
# OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput" makes the ownership and mode apply to the
# device node systemd-udevd creates ahead of the module being loaded. Without
# it the permissions only land after something has already opened the device
# as root, which on a desktop is never.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", GROUP="input", MODE="0660", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
